Resolution Relativity

Your dinosaur observation points at a real epistemic asymmetry.

We sometimes feel more comfortable saying something about 100 million years ago than about an event 15 years ago because distance in time is not the same thing as epistemic difficulty.

The better quantity is something like:

Confidence \sim
\frac{\text{usable evidence bandwidth}}
{\text{resolution demanded by the question}}

For dinosaurs, evidence bandwidth is awful—but the questions are often coarse:

Did this lineage exist? Roughly when? What did the skeleton look like?

Stone preserves certain variables extraordinarily well over geological time.

For something last Tuesday, we may ask:

Who knew what, at exactly what time, with what motive, and why?

There may be a million traces, but also contradiction, incentives, missing messages, privacy, deception, ambiguity, and an enormously higher demanded resolution.

So:

\boxed{\text{Each timescale has its own native resolution and evidentiary affordances.}}

Deep time gives low-resolution / high-durability evidence.

Recent time gives potentially high-resolution / high-noise evidence.

And that’s another fractal rule: you cannot demand microscope resolution from a telescope image merely because the telescope sees farther.

Call that Resolution Relativity or Temporal Resolution Matching.

